## This repository's agent infrastructure

- `.claude/README.md` — component reference: what each agent/command/
  skill/hook below does and why, one line each.
- `.claude/agents/` — subagents (`triage`, `architect`, `researcher`,
  `coder`, `quickfix`, `reviewer`), each with a fixed model. `quickfix`
  is `coder`'s cheaper sibling for the trivial tier `triage` classifies
  — same expectations, lighter model.
- `.claude/commands/` — slash commands (`/spec`, `/plan`, `/tasks`,
  `/implement`, `/review`, `/adr`), the pipeline's deterministic entry
  points; `/quick` for a change too small for the full chain (trivial →
  straight fix, standard → one-file lite spec); `/update-docs` to bring
  architecture docs back in line with the code (census projects);
  `/metrics` for what each feature cost; `/reconcile` to run an on-demand spec-vs-code fidelity sweep
  against any already-`implemented` spec, any time; `/worktree` to
  isolate a task in parallel from `origin/{{MAIN_INTEGRATION_BRANCH}}`;
  `/setup-framework` to adopt or maintain this framework itself
  (resolving `{{PLACEHOLDER}}`s, installing recommended global plugins
  — see `docs/workflow/plugin-integrations.md` — and merging
  scaffolding files like `.gitignore`/`.mcp.json`).
- `.claude/skills/` — knowledge reusable across stages. Ships with
  `adr-writing`, `skill-authoring`, and `plugin-awareness` (what the
  framework's own process needs) — add your own for anything
  project-specific that applies "at any stage" (a scaffolding
  checklist, a structural invariant like a required isolation pattern,
  a sensitive-data rule, etc.), following `skill-authoring/SKILL.md`'s
  format. Tag each with `applies_to: [agent-name, ...]` in its
  frontmatter — that's how a project signals a new skill to the agents
  that need it (edit the skill once, not every agent that should check
  it). `.claude/skills/README.md` is kept in sync automatically from
  those tags; each pipeline agent checks it for entries tagged with its
  own name before starting a task.
- `.claude/settings.example.json` — sample hook for the deterministic
  gate (build/lint/tests), plus a `SessionStart` check that flags a
  missing language-LSP plugin for this repo's stack.
- `.mcp.json.example` ships Context7 pre-configured (up-to-date,
  version-specific library docs on demand) — rename to `.mcp.json` to
  use it; `researcher` prefers it over broad web search for known
  libraries. Add further MCP entries the same way as your project
  needs them (e.g. an org-specific one like Azure DevOps — see
  `docs/workflow/plugin-integrations.md` for a copy-paste example).

Full rationale → framework ADR 0001.

## Conventions for any agent working in this repo

1. **The constitution is supreme** — `docs/constitution-baseline.md`
   plus `docs/constitution.md`, whichever exist. No spec,
   technical plan, or code may knowingly contradict a principle there.
   A conflict gets flagged explicitly, never silently worked around —
   see the file's own Governance section for how it's amended (never a
   silent edit).
2. **Never duplicate information across files.** If a fact already
   exists in another doc, reference its path — don't copy the content.
3. **Every new architecture decision becomes an ADR** in
   `docs/decisions/`, sequentially numbered, immutable once accepted
   (changes become a new ADR that supersedes the old one, never a
   silent edit).
4. **Product specs** (features validated with the stakeholder) live
   outside this architecture docs set — see
   `docs/product/requirements-template.md` for the format used before
   something becomes an implementation task, including how a spec
   auto-tags its own `area` and lineage (`relates_to`), and how its
   "## Reconciliation" section tracks spec-vs-code fidelity as tasks
   are implemented. Specs get a `.validation-{{STAKEHOLDER_LANG_CODE}}.md`
   companion for their review, if the language split applies.
5. When generating code, the agent should cite which ADR or
   architecture doc grounds the implementation decision, when
   applicable.
6. **Architecture docs describe, they don't prescribe.** An `active`
   architecture doc is ground truth; a `draft` one is advisory until
   `/update-docs promote` verifies it. A documented Pitfall, Finding or
   deviation is never a pattern to copy, and no doc states inventory
   counts — see `docs/workflow/living-architecture-docs.md`.
7. **Context-engineering discipline**: every doc carries a
   `context_budget` estimate in its frontmatter. When choosing which
   doc to load, prefer the smallest one that answers the current
   question; load the overview only when nothing narrower applies.
